          "def-oriented-smooth-manifold-and-oriented-chart": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-smooth-differential-k-form": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-wedge-product-of-differential-forms": "mathematical-premise",
          "lem-gr-metric-volume-form-patching-and-face-normal": "mathematical-premise",
          "lem-the-invariant-exterior-derivative-formula-is-c-infinity-multilinear": "mathematical-premise",
          "prop-differential-forms-form-a-graded-commutative-algebra": "mathematical-premise",
          "prop-local-coordinate-expression-for-a-differential-form": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-clairaut-schwarz-mixed-partials": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-local-coordinate-formula-for-the-exterior-derivative": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-the-exterior-derivative-is-a-graded-derivation": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "inventory_expansion_required": false,
        "statement": "Let $(M,g,N,o)$ have the oriented, time-oriented Lorentzian structure and volume form of [[def-emg-lorentzian-forms]]. On decomposable $k$-covectors define the induced exterior pairing by\n\n$$\\langle\\alpha_1\\wedge\\cdots\\wedge\\alpha_k,\\beta_1\\wedge\\cdots\\wedge\\beta_k\\rangle_g:=\\det\\bigl(g^{-1}(\\alpha_i,\\beta_j)\\bigr),$$\n\nand extend it bilinearly. For $0\\le k\\le4$, its coordinate expression is the pointwise exterior metric on $k$-covectors\n\n$$\\langle\\alpha,\\beta\\rangle_g:=\\frac1{k!}\\alpha_{a_1\\cdots a_k}\\beta^{a_1\\cdots a_k},$$\n\nwhere indices are raised by $g^{-1}$. There is a unique smooth bundle map $\\star_g:\\Omega^k(M)\\to\\Omega^{4-k}(M)$ characterized by\n\n$$\\alpha\\wedge\\star_g\\beta=\\langle\\alpha,\\beta\\rangle_g\\,\\mathrm{vol}_g$$\n\nat every point. In a positively oriented $g$-orthonormal coframe $(e^0,e^1,e^2,e^3)$, put $\\epsilon_0=-1$ and $\\epsilon_1=\\epsilon_2=\\epsilon_3=1$. For an increasing index set $I\\subseteq\\{0,1,2,3\\}$, let $I^c$ be its increasing complement, let $s(I,I^c)$ be the sign of the permutation $(I,I^c)$ to $(0,1,2,3)$, and set $\\epsilon_I:=\\prod_{i\\in I}\\epsilon_i$. Then\n\n$$\\star_g e^I=\\epsilon_I s(I,I^c)e^{I^c}.$$\n\nConsequently, on $k$-forms,\n\n$$\\star_g^2=(-1)^{k(4-k)+1}\\operatorname{id}.$$\n\nIn particular $\\star_g^2=-\\operatorname{id}$ on two-forms. For a two-form $F$, the component formula in a positively oriented chart is\n\n$$({\\star_gF})_{ab}=\\frac12(\\mathrm{vol}_g)_{abcd}F^{cd}.$$\n\nThe exterior derivative in [[def-exterior-derivative-by-the-invariant-vector-field-formula]] has the local coordinate formula of [[thm-local-coordinate-formula-for-the-exterior-derivative]], satisfies $d^2=0$, and obeys the graded product rule of [[thm-the-exterior-derivative-is-a-graded-derivation]].",
        "strategy": "Construct the pointwise star in a positively oriented Lorentz-orthonormal coframe, prove its defining identity and uniqueness, then derive smoothness, the star-square sign, the component formula and d-squared.",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "ai-altered",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "David Tong, General Relativity, Cambridge Part III lecture notes, section 3.1.3, The Hodge Dual, equations (3.97)-(3.98)",
              "url": "https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/tong/gr/grhtml/S3.html",
              "locator": "Section 3.1.3, equations (3.97)-(3.98): Hodge star from the metric and orientation, with the Lorentzian signature factor."
            },
            {
              "title": "H. S. Reall, Part III General Relativity, Cambridge, October 12 2022 revision, sections 8.1 and 8.5, equations (8.1)-(8.9) and (8.52)-(8.56)",
              "url": "https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/hsr1000/part3_gr_lectures.pdf",
              "locator": "Section 8.1, equations (8.1)-(8.9): forms, wedge and exterior derivative; section 8.5, equations (8.52)-(8.56): Lorentzian volume, Hodge definition and star square."
            }
          ]
        },
        "library": "mathematics",
        "proof_strategy": "direct"
      },
      {
        "id": "lem-emg-conformal-hodge-multiplier",
        "kind": "lemma",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "title": "Conformal Hodge scaling in every degree",
        "reference": "items/lem-emg-conformal-hodge-multiplier.md#Statement",
        "design_state": "Fully authored draft with complete local argument; independent review remains pending.",
        "dependency_level": 2,
        "deps": [
          "def-countable-choice",
          "def-emg-lorentzian-forms",
          "lem-emg-lorentz-hodge-calculus"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-emg-lorentzian-forms": "mathematical-premise",
          "lem-emg-lorentz-hodge-calculus": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "inventory_expansion_required": false,
        "statement": "Let $(M,g,N,o)$ be as in [[def-emg-lorentzian-forms]], let $\\Omega:M\\to(0,\\infty)$ be a smooth dimensionless function, and set $\\widetilde g=\\Omega^2g$ with the same orientation $o$. For every smooth $k$-form $\\beta$, $0\\le k\\le4$,\n\n$$\\star_{\\widetilde g}\\beta=\\Omega^{4-2k}\\star_g\\beta.$$\n\nIn degree $2$ the Hodge star is unchanged. This is a statement about the Hodge operators of two conformally related Lorentz metrics; it asserts no conformal invariance of any curvature or field equation.",
        "strategy": "Scale the inverse metric and oriented volume, substitute into the unique Hodge characterization and use wedge nondegeneracy.",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "ai-altered",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "David Tong, General Relativity, Cambridge Part III lecture notes, section 3.1.3, The Hodge Dual, equations (3.97)-(3.98)",
              "url": "https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/tong/gr/grhtml/S3.html",
              "locator": "Section 3.1.3, equations (3.97)-(3.98): Hodge star defined by the metric and oriented volume; the conformal multiplier is derived locally in the item."
            },
            {
              "title": "H. S. Reall, Part III General Relativity, Cambridge, October 12 2022 revision, section 8.5, equations (8.52)-(8.56)",
              "url": "https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/hsr1000/part3_gr_lectures.pdf",
              "locator": "Section 8.5, equations (8.52)-(8.56): metric volume and Hodge star in Lorentzian signature; the all-degree conformal scaling is derived locally."
            }
          ]
        },
        "library": "mathematics",
        "proof_strategy": "direct"
      },
      {
        "id": "lem-emg-observer-field-decomposition",
        "kind": "lemma",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "title": "Observer electric/magnetic reconstruction and invariants",
        "reference": "items/lem-emg-observer-field-decomposition.md#Statement",
        "design_state": "Fully authored draft with complete local argument; independent review remains pending.",
        "dependency_level": 2,
        "deps": [
          "def-countable-choice",
          "def-emg-lorentzian-forms",
          "def-gr-inverse-metric-and-musical-isomorphisms",
          "def-gr-observer-and-rest-space",
          "lem-emg-lorentz-hodge-calculus",
          "lem-gr-observer-projection-decomposition",
          "prop-differential-forms-form-a-graded-commutative-algebra"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-emg-lorentzian-forms": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-gr-inverse-metric-and-musical-isomorphisms": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-gr-observer-and-rest-space": "mathematical-premise",
          "lem-emg-lorentz-hodge-calculus": "mathematical-premise",
          "lem-gr-observer-projection-decomposition": "mathematical-premise",
          "prop-differential-forms-form-a-graded-commutative-algebra": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "inventory_expansion_required": false,
        "statement": "Let $U\\subseteq M$ be an open set with smooth observer congruence $n$, let $F$ be a smooth real two-form on $U$, and let $c>0$ be the coordinate conversion parameter of [[def-emg-lorentzian-forms]]. Define covectors\n\n$$E_a:=cF_{ab}n^b,\\qquad B_a:=-({\\star_gF})_{ab}n^b.$$\n\nHere $c$ is the positive coordinate-conversion parameter of [[def-emg-lorentzian-forms]], with SI unit $\\mathrm m\\,\\mathrm s^{-1}$ in its length-coordinate convention. The names electric and magnetic distinguish these two covectors; for arbitrary mathematical $F$ no physical field unit or measurement is assigned.\n\nThey annihilate $n$. On the rest covector space, use the positive inner product induced by $g^{-1}$ and write $\\langle E,B\\rangle_n:=g^{-1}(E,B)$. Then\n\n$$F=n^\\flat\\wedge E/c+\\star_g(n^\\flat\\wedge B),$$\n\n$$F_{ab}F^{ab}=2\\bigl(\\langle B,B\\rangle_n-\\langle E,E\\rangle_n/c^2\\bigr),\\qquad F\\wedge F=-\\frac{2}{c}\\langle E,B\\rangle_n\\,\\mathrm{vol}_g.$$\n\nEquivalently, in any positively oriented future tetrad with $E_0=n$, and with $\\epsilon_{123}=+1$ in its oriented spatial triad, the components satisfy $F_{0i}=-E_i/c$ and $F_{ij}=\\epsilon_{ijk}B_k$. These are pointwise mathematical decompositions of an arbitrary two-form; no Maxwell equation or physical observation is assumed.",
        "strategy": "Use a local observer-adapted tetrad to compute all six independent two-form components, then contract and wedge to obtain the two scalar invariants.",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "ai-altered",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "David Tong, General Relativity, Cambridge Part III lecture notes, section 2.4.2, The Electromagnetic Field, equation (2.87), and section 3.1.3, The Hodge Dual",
              "url": "https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/tong/gr/grhtml/S2.html",
              "locator": "Section 2.4.2, equation (2.87): field-strength components in a Minkowski frame. The curved observer decomposition and its invariants are proved locally."
            },
            {
              "title": "H. S. Reall, Part III General Relativity, Cambridge, October 12 2022 revision, sections 2.2 and 8.5, equations (2.9)-(2.10) and (8.55)-(8.56)",
              "url": "https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/hsr1000/part3_gr_lectures.pdf",
              "locator": "Section 2.2, equations (2.9)-(2.10): Lorentzian orthonormal bases; section 8.5, equations (8.55)-(8.56): oriented Hodge star and its Lorentzian square."
            }
          ]
        },
        "library": "mathematics",
        "proof_strategy": "direct"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
